# Rift Roulette — Development Rules

Source of truth for Rift Roulette work. Mirrored as Cursor always-apply rules in
`.cursor/rules/riftroulette.mdc`. Handoff context lives in
`Bublock/RiftRoulette/reference/chat-handoff.md`. Resources and discoveries live
in `Bublock/RiftRoulette/reference/resources.md`. Rebuild roadmap lives in
`Bublock/RiftRoulette/reference/master-plan.md`.

These rules govern documentation, architecture, execution mode, and logging.
They do not authorize inventing Deadworks APIs.

---

## 0. Workspace layout

```text
deadworks/                 # Cursor workspace root
  lib/                     # shared libraries (keep)
  ref/                     # shared reference assemblies (keep)
  Directory.Build.Targets  # shared deploy (opt-in)
  archive/                 # frozen known-good oracles
  Bublock/                 # active multi-plugin home
    Shared/                # Shared.projitems: Auth, Cheats, ExecutionMode, Logging
    Modules/               # reusable game-agnostic modules (<Name>/<Name>.projitems)
      WorldText/           # boards / in-game text (dw_wt_*)
      Movement/            # named locations, teleports (dw_mv_*)
      Hud/                 # on-screen announcement banners (dw_hud_*)
      Loadout/             # stored hero builds, give a loadout (dw_loadout_*)
      Restraint/           # silence / disarm / no melee until released (dw_restrain*)
      Queue/               # reusable player join queue (no commands)
      Spectate/            # spectator camera: follow a player, park a free camera (no commands)
    RiftRoulette/            # game mode DLL
      Session/             # session lifecycle lines in the master log
      Lobby/ Draft/ Rift/  # game-specific plugin classes
      Round/               # parity round composer (Rift + Draft + Movement ops) + watch spot
      GameLoop/            # match loop + MatchConfig (hero mode, format) + auto-start
      RandomMode/          # random hero + build every round
      Stats/               # match kills / deaths / assists, stats boards
      Balance/             # auto-balance swaps between rounds
      Duel/                # 1v1 mode: copied build, queue, winner stays on
      SelfTest/            # patch-day self-test of every game dependency (dw_selftest_*)
    DevTools/              # discovery / diagnostics
    CleanSlate/            # map cleanup helpers
    Tests/                 # local xUnit tests (never deployed)
    logs/                  # Cursor pull root (<Dll>/ subfolders)
    server-data/           # pulled access.json (bans / whitelist), git-ignored
    scripts/update.sh      # build-all (never deploys)
    scripts/deploy.sh      # backup server DLLs, then upload (needs --confirm)
    scripts/rollback.sh    # re-upload a deploy backup (<stamp>)
    scripts/test.sh        # run local tests
    scripts/pull-logs.sh   # read-only pull: server logs into logs/, access.json into server-data/
    scripts/fetch-builds.py # refresh Modules/Loadout/Data/hero-builds.json (offline)
    scripts/walker-souls.py # print loadout item-limit breakpoints from real matches (offline)
    scripts/check-spots.py # check the per-slot spots against the map mesh (offline)
    scripts/patch-baseline.sh # save a known-good snapshot before a game patch
    scripts/patch-check.py # diff game data / API vs that snapshot (offline)
  server-backups/          # deploy.sh backups (<UTC stamp>/), outside git
```

- **New plugin work** lives under `Bublock/` (many plugins).
- `archive/` holds the frozen known-good plugins, the oracle for parity checks.
  Never edit it.
- Shared `lib/` and `ref/` stay at the workspace root for the agent and builds.
- Shared conventions (logging, auth, docs, Clean/Debug) apply across Bublock plugins.
- `Shared/` and `Modules/` are **not** plugins on their own. They are compiled
  into consuming DLLs as source via `.projitems` imports. Never ship them as
  separate library DLLs in the server plugins folder (the loader treats every
  `plugins/*.dll` as a plugin, and only discovers commands inside the loaded DLL).
- `Modules/` MUST NOT depend on Rift Roulette. Game-specific constants and
  logic live under `RiftRoulette/`.

---

## 0.1. Git hygiene — Cursor IDE files

Assume the git repo is rooted at `Bublock/`.

- Never commit Cursor IDE paths (`.cursor/`, or any other Cursor-local metadata).
- Committed rules source of truth is `RiftRoulette/reference/.rules` (under Bublock).
- Workspace `deadworks/.cursor/rules/riftroulette.mdc` is a **local** always-apply
  mirror only: regenerate it from `.rules` when rules change; do not commit it.
- `Bublock/.gitignore` MUST keep `.cursor/` excluded (along with build outputs
  and pulled logs under `logs/` / `RiftRoulette/logs/`).
- Cursor commit/PR attribution trailers are allowed; do not strip them.
- The repo is public on GitHub. Server SFTP host, port and user live only in
  git-ignored `scripts/server.env` (template `server.env.example`); never
  put them, passwords or keychain values in committed files. The public
  join address in `README.md` is fine.
- License: PolyForm Noncommercial 1.0.0 (`LICENSE`). Noncommercial changes,
  new works, and sharing are allowed. Future versions may be relicensed;
  merged outside contributions need their authors' agreement first.
  Third-party data (hero builds, map extracts, game data) is not covered by it.

---

## 0.2. Master plan — one stage at a time

- Rebuild work follows `Bublock/RiftRoulette/reference/master-plan.md`.
- Pull **one** unchecked stage, make a focused Cursor plan for that stage only,
  implement, verify, then mark the stage done in `master-plan.md`.
- Do **not** skip ahead into a full lifecycle rewrite or multi-stage mega-PR.
- Gameplay stages are split into sub-stages (13a, 13b, ...), each with its
  own plan.
- Do **not** SFTP/push any Bublock plugin to the live server without the
  user's explicit approval for that upload. The only push path is
  `Bublock/scripts/deploy.sh --confirm`, which backs up the server DLLs to
  `deadworks/server-backups/<stamp>/` first (`--no-backup` only when the
  user says so); `rollback.sh <stamp>` restores them. `update.sh` is
  build-only. Every deploy also runs `pull-logs.sh` before uploading
  (logs, and `access.json` into `Bublock/server-data/` and the stamp
  folder), so the ban list and whitelist have a local copy until they
  move to database storage. Never commit `server-data/` (Steam IDs).
  To change the live lists, edit `server-data/access.json` and deploy with
  `--push-access` (the server's old copy stays in the stamp folder).
- When a stage changes architecture decisions, update `master-plan.md` and this
  `.rules` file in the same change.
- Round flow is composed by `RiftRoulette/Round/RoundFlow` from Rift, Draft,
  and Movement ops. The lifecycle calls it in Clean mode; admin commands
  call the same methods in Debug mode.
- The match loop (`RiftRoulette/GameLoop/MatchService`) is the lifecycle: it
  starts rounds through `RoundFlow` and learns each result through the
  `RiftRoundSteps.RoundEnded` step. Players see match state on the HUD
  banner (`Modules/Hud`), not chat spam.
- `GameLoop/MatchConfig` holds the match settings (hero mode `random` or
  `draft`, format `continuous`). In Random mode `RandomModeService` gives
  every player a random hero and one of its stored top builds each
  intermission (through `Modules/Loadout`); draft code stays but its boards
  and pick commands are off. Build data is fetched offline by
  `scripts/fetch-builds.py` and embedded in the DLL; the server never calls
  the Deadlock API.
- Loadouts (`Modules/Loadout`, `LoadoutPlanner.Plan`) shop the build in
  order up to the item limit for the cap (`Modules/Loadout/ItemSlots`: 9,
  then 10 / 11 / 12 from 16,000 / 22,000 / 28,000 souls, when real teams'
  Walkers fall; `scripts/walker-souls.py`) and never go over the soul cap
  (`/loadout_cap`). The HUD always shows 12 slots (every flex slot open:
  `Lobby/FlexSlots` writes `m_nFlexSlotsUnlocked = 15` on both team
  entities; the demo convar alone does nothing); never cap items by
  locking slots. Once the limit is reached they sell by the build's sell
  priority, else the cheapest and earliest, for pricier items. Only at 12
  items are empty slots filled from the build's optional items (most
  expensive first) and held component items upgraded while the cap
  allows. The hero's level, boons and ability ranks come from
  the cap, never from the items' value.
- `Stats/StatsService` counts match kills, deaths and assists
  from `player_death` and draws them on the Sapphire / Amber boards (Random
  mode). `Balance/BalanceService` decides swaps at the start of each Random
  intermission, before heroes are drawn. New connections join the smaller
  team (`Lobby/TeamBalance`). In Random mode a menu hero change kills the
  player, who respawns with the assigned hero and build; that death is not
  counted.
- Random mode fighting teams are always even: with an odd count (3+) one
  player sits out each round (`RandomMode/BenchRule`, a `PlayerQueue`
  rotation owned by `RandomModeService`). The bench player gets no hero and
  no pick, so `RoundFlow` leaves them up top, restrained. Joins and leaves
  in an intermission sub the bench player in; never give a bench player a
  pick without evening the teams.
- Betting (Random mode, `Betting/BettingService`): 100 starting betting
  souls (a separate balance, not in-game souls; player text always says
  souls, never chips; they never add fighting power), 100 per credited
  kill, 50 per credited assist, all-in bets on the next round from the start of the intermission
  (default 5 s, `MatchService.DefaultIntermissionSeconds`)
  until 10 s into the round (`BettingService.LingerSeconds`), a win doubles
  the stake, no result refunds. Fighters bet only on their own team. Bets
  come from chat lines that are exactly `sapphire` / `amber`
  (`BettingPlugin.OnChatMessage`) or `/bet`. Betting messages are chat
  only, never a banner. The leaderboard is `BoardLayout.Back`, the empty
  side of the watch spot. Souls buy only two things. A hero reservation
  (`/reserve <hero>`, `RandomMode/HeroReservations`): 1,000 souls for the
  player's next 3 fighting rounds, a waiting line per hero, and each round
  the first fighter in a hero's line gets it (never two on one hero).
  Reserved heroes go into `HeroDraw.Draw` as fixed heroes; everyone else
  stays random. Rounds sat out don't count; no refunds. The waiting line
  never names the holder. A hero ban (`/heroban <hero>`,
  `RandomMode/HeroBans`): 1,000 souls, the hero leaves the next draw for
  both teams for one round; one ban per team per round, first come (a
  teammate is told the team's ban, not charged). The other team's ban is
  never revealed early; at round start everyone gets the chat line
  `Banned this round: <heroes>` (heroes only, never who, never a banner).
  A ban on a reserved hero uses one of the holder's rounds (random hero,
  private chat line).
- 1v1 mode has no team score: the only score is the best-streak
  leaderboard (`Duel/StreakBoard`), shown on both stats boards and in
  `/score`.
- Join access lives in `bublock/access.json` on the server (banned /
  allowed Steam64 IDs, open or private). `Lobby/AccessService` checks it
  in `OnClientConnect` before the admin seat rule; a ban wins over
  everything. The file never grants admin (that stays `AdminAuth`).
- Banned players are never kicked on the spot: `Lobby/BanStatueService`
  turns them to stone up top (out of the game, restrained, the
  `statueModifier` from `access.json`) and kicks them 30 s after a live
  ban, 10 s after a reconnect. Each reconnect is a strike
  (`Lobby/BanJoinRule`): refused 10 min, then 30 min, then until restart.
  Statues are not participants. Never kick inside `OnClientConnect` /
  `OnClientFullConnect`; kick from a timer. A new statue gets the HUD
  banner `<name> is banned` with a stone joke under it, and the stream
  camera follows it for 10 s (`StreamCam.ShowStatue`).
- Build against the server's Deadworks: after any Deadworks update copy
  its `managed/DeadworksManaged.Api.dll` into `lib/`. A hook that uses a
  member whose type changed on the server throws before its first line on
  every call (only the host console shows it). Map-change disconnects
  (`ClientDisconnectedEvent.IsMapChange`) are not leaves; never remove
  those players.
- Pausing follows private mode (`Lobby/PauseGuard.FollowAccess`): on
  every load it is on when `access.json` is private (organised events) and
  off when open; `/access_mode open|private` switches it with the mode.
  While off: pause convars 0, pause console commands and both pause net
  messages blocked, and a game paused anyway gets the server `pause`
  toggle. `/pause_allow on|off` overrides it until the next load or mode
  change. Never set `citadel_pause_count` or
  `citadel_num_team_pauses_allowed` to 0 (0 means unlimited).
- `GameLoop/AutoStartService` starts the match (Clean) when 2
  human players are connected and ends it when fewer than 2 remain, checked
  2 s after every join (`CheckSoon`), on every disconnect and 3 s after
  load. A disconnect never starts a match (it crashed the server with the
  leaving controller still listed); never start a match inside
  `OnClientFullConnect` (the hero swaps are lost). `/match_auto off` hands
  control back to `/match_start` / `/match_end`. A lone player waiting for
  a match always gets told why: a chat line (never a banner; banners go by
  too fast) on join and every 30 s (`AutoStartService.RemindWaiting`).
- The server takes 13 connections (12 shown); the 13th is an
  admin-only seat on the spectator side (`Lobby/AdminSeat`, console
  `dw_seat_spec` / `dw_seat_play`). Admins are seated on every connect.
  `seat_spec` is `ConsoleOnly` and works any time: `ChangeTeam(1, false)`
  then `MakeObserver()` on the next tick. Never spectate with `ChangeTeam`
  alone (the hero pawn stays and the client drops). Every player list the game uses
  (teams, heroes, rounds, stats, auto-start) comes from
  `Lobby/Participants.Humans()`, which leaves out bots, seated admins and
  a player whose disconnect is being handled (the disconnect hook still
  lists the leaving controller); never build a new ad-hoc human list. A seated admin defaults to
  spectating on connect (`AdminSeat.Sit`). Roaming is manual
  (`/seat_roam` / `dw_seat_roam`): Abrams on Amber facing the welcome sign
  (`WatchLayout.WelcomeFront`; back to the watch slot if they fall), not
  restrained, invisible (`modifier_invis`, one 3600 s modifier put back
  when it runs out and on respawn / reload, never every frame), and works
  while players are connected (game chat). `dw_seat_spec` while roaming
  returns to spectating. Roaming admins stay seated (never participants).
  There is no auto roam↔spectate sync on join/leave.
- Auto restart (`Lobby/AutoRestartService`, rule `AutoRestartRule`):
  after hours up the server can stop letting new players finish joining
  (players in keep playing). Every 60 s, with no participants, it reloads
  the map (`Server.ChangeLevel(Server.MapName)`, never `quit`) after a
  stuck join (connected, never full-connected: left, or 3 min) or 3 h up;
  never within 10 min of a map start. Clients reconnect by themselves
  (`IsMapChangeReconnect`). Map starts (`OnStartupServer`) must reset
  per-map state: plugin statics survive a map change, pawns do not
  (`AdminSeat.ResetForMap`).
- Join budget (`Lobby/MapRefreshService`, rule `MapRefreshRule`): hero
  swaps grow the string tables sent to every joiner, and past 512 KB no
  one can join. Each Random mode round adds its fighter count; at a
  scored round end after 160 fighter-rounds since the map start, everyone
  gets a chat line that they will reconnect (never a banner), the match
  ends, and the map reloads 10 s later; auto-start is held until then and
  starts a fresh match when players are back. Never end a round early for
  it, and never raise the budget without new join-size data.
- Hero mode `duel` (`/match_mode 1v1`) copies one player's exact
  hero state (`Modules/Loadout` `LoadoutSnapshot`) onto both players with
  `/duel_copy <slot>` and locks them to it (`Lobby/HeroLock`, one instance
  per mode), re-applied every intermission. Code that means "the draft is
  off" checks `MatchConfig.UsesDraft`, not `IsRandom`.
- Every player the game sends up top is restrained
  (`Modules/Restraint`: real modifier `modifier_citadel_silenced` plus
  `Silenced` / `ItemsDisabled` / `ShootingDisabled` / `MeleeDisabled` /
  `IgnoredByNpcTargeting` states set every frame) until
  `RoundFlow.MoveTeamsToRift` releases them and heals them to full.
  Restrained players take no damage (`GameLoopPlugin.OnTakeDamage` returns
  `Stop` when `RestraintService.IsRestrainedPawn`), so leftover turrets and
  troopers can't hurt anyone up top. Never use disarm
  (`modifier_citadel_disarmed` / `Disarmed`): it also blocks reloading.
- "Up top" is `Round/WatchSpot`, above the rift being fought
  (running) or the next one (idle), per `WatchSpotRule`. Never teleport to
  the fixed `draft` spot; call `WatchSpot.SendUp` (teleport + restrain).
  The spot and the boards only move when players are sent back up after a
  round (round end passes `RiftService.NextSide` explicitly).
- Per-slot spots: every teleport up top or into a rift goes to the player's
  own spot, `Round/SlotSpots` (`Watch` / `Fight`) with `player.Slot`: an
  anchor from `RiftRouletteLocations` plus that slot's (forward, right, up)
  offset from `Round/Data/spots.json`. Never teleport a group to one point.
  Move a group by moving its anchor; after changing an anchor or
  `spots.json`, run `scripts/check-spots.py` (all spots must pass) and
  `/spots_walk` in game.
- Game patches: after any Deadlock or Deadworks update, follow
  `reference/patch-day.md` (`patch-check.py`, rebuild against the new
  `lib/`, `dw_selftest_run`). A new game dependency (convar, entity name,
  schema field, modifier, event) goes into `SelfTest/GameDependencies` or a
  `SelfTestService` check and into `patch-day.md` section 5 in the same
  change. Set convars with `ServerConVars.TrySet`, never a bare
  `ConVar.Find(...)?.SetInt`.
- 1v1 mode is winner-stays-on over a `Modules/Queue`
  `PlayerQueue` owned by `DuelService`: the first two queued are the
  fighters (`IsFighter`), only they get the build and lock and move into
  the rift; the loser goes to the back; auto-start counts the queue in 1v1
  mode. New "wait your turn" features reuse `PlayerQueue`.
- Hot reload: an upload hot-reloads each DLL, which runs
  `OnLoad(isReload: true)` but not `OnStartupServer` and drops timers from
  the previous load. Startup work that matters (CleanSlate cleanup, server
  convars, boards) must also run in `OnLoad` when `isReload` is true.
  A reload also ends a running match (`MatchService.State` starts empty),
  and with no match `SoulRule` blocks nothing: after an upload mid-match,
  run `/match_start` again (auto-start only does it with 2+ players).
- Buying: CleanSlate disables every shop; buying anywhere is on only in 1v1
  setup (1v1 mode, no match). Only `GameLoop/ShopAccess` sets
  `citadel_allow_purchasing_anywhere`. Items reach players with `AddItem`.
- Souls: while a match runs every earned soul gain is blocked
  (`GameLoop/SoulRule`, applied in `GameLoopPlugin.OnModifyCurrency`);
  power comes only from the round's build. `ECheats`, `EStartingAmount`
  and `EItemSale` pass so loadouts still set gold.
- Rift troopers: round end and cancel remove every `npc_trooper` (lane
  troopers are off) and sweep again 5 s and 10 s later; the cash-in wave
  keeps spawning after the round ends. Never sweep at rift start.
- `Round/WatchGuard` sends restrained players who drop 300 units below the
  watch spot back up (Unstuck cannot be blocked yet).
- Banners and chat: only what players need (match start, round result,
  their hero and build with its soul value, `Round N` / score 3 s before
  each round). No debug-style banners or chat lines (item counts, "abilities
  on", cleanup notices); details go to the logs. Fold a new message into an
  existing banner when both would show at the same moment.
- If a round's new rift does not spawn in time, `RiftService` uses a rift
  already on the map (`FindRiftOnMap`, side from `RiftSides.TryMatch`,
  else `Nearest`); every go-live goes through `GoLive`, so teams, watch
  spot and next side follow the rift actually used. A live rift is its
  `citadel_koth_cashin` (the spawner removes itself about 1 s after
  spawning); a cash-in already up before the forced spawn is a leftover
  and is adopted right away.
- Stream camera: a seated admin's camera is run by `Lobby/StreamCam`
  (every 2 s and on deaths) through `Modules/Spectate`; no command
  needed. Players to watch: follow one, cut to the killer. Nobody to
  watch: park at the saved framing for `WatchSpot.Side`
  (`StreamFramingStore`, `bublock/streamcam.json`; default straight down
  264 above the main `WatchSpot.Location` anchor). Moving the camera
  after that park and letting go saves the new framing, relative to the
  side's anchor (`StreamFraming`), so it mirrors to the other side;
  `/spec_reset` forgets it. The server's observer mode does not switch
  the client: every park goes through `SpectateService.Park` (teleport,
  then the angle later) and only moves an admin already in fly cam (C;
  the client refuses `spec_*` commands from the server, so none are
  sent). Fly cam is `SpectateService.IsFlyCam` (observer `Roaming`, no
  target). The view angle is `CBasePlayerPawn.v_angle`
  (`SpectateService.ViewAngle`; the observer pawn has no `EyeAngles`).
  Never move the camera while the admin moves it in fly cam (the client
  crashed): no camera call on an update where the view moved over 50
  units or 3 degrees (`SpectateRule.HandMoved`). Never use
  `IsValidObserverTarget` (it rejects team 3, Sapphire). A hot reload
  re-seats every connected admin (`AdminSeat.Restore`).
  Never follow a hero spawned less than 5 s ago (`StreamCam.FollowGrace`).

---

## 1. Colocated file documentation

- Every source file `Foo.cs` MUST have a sibling `Foo.md`.
- The colocated doc describes behavior succinctly and exactly:
  - what the file does
  - inputs / outputs
  - side effects
  - invariants
  - dangerous Deadworks constraints (if any)
- **Edit workflow (mandatory):**
  1. Read the colocated `.md` first.
  2. Change the code.
  3. Update the colocated `.md` last in the same change.
- Never leave documentation stale relative to the code it describes.
- Do not ship a new source file without its sibling doc.

---

## 2. Component / feature documentation

- Each component or feature folder MUST have a `FEATURE.md`
  (examples: `Shared/FEATURE.md`, `Modules/WorldText/FEATURE.md`,
  `RiftRoulette/Draft/FEATURE.md`, `RiftRoulette/Rift/FEATURE.md`).
- Feature docs use the same read-first / update-last workflow as file docs.
- A `FEATURE.md` MUST cover:
  - purpose of the component
  - public operations it exposes
  - state it owns (if any)
  - which command/operation units compose it
  - how it relates to game lifecycle vs admin/player commands

---

## 3. Command catalogs

Maintain living catalogs under Bublock reference. Update them whenever
commands are added, renamed, restricted, or removed.

- `Bublock/RiftRoulette/reference/user-commands.md` — player-facing commands
- `Bublock/RiftRoulette/reference/admin-commands.md` — admin / debug / force commands

Each catalog entry MUST include:

- invocation (chat form and/or `dw_` console form)
- who may use it
- which core operation(s) it calls
- clean vs debug behavior
- expected side effects

Do not invent undocumented commands in catalogs. Populate entries from
verified source.

---

## 4. Resource discovery

Whenever research finds something **highly useful** or **difficult to find**,
add an entry to `Bublock/RiftRoulette/reference/resources.md` under
**Discoveries** in the same change.

- Prefer linking official docs/schema over restating them.
- Discoveries is for non-obvious verified knowledge (APIs, fields, pitfalls,
  sequences, convars).
- Detailed earlier session narrative remains in `chat-handoff.md`.
- Keep this `.rules` file and `.cursor/rules/riftroulette.mdc` in sync when
  this policy changes.

---

## 5. Clean mode vs debug mode

Every operation and command MUST support an execution mode (explicit
parameter or ambient context flag).

| Invoker | Mode |
|---------|------|
| Manual admin command | Debug ON |
| Game lifecycle orchestration | Debug OFF (clean) |

Rules:

- **Debug ON** enables deeper subsystem log detail and extra diagnostic
  fields for that invocation’s scope.
- **Debug OFF (clean)** keeps lifecycle noise low: Information+ for that
  run’s detail stream unless elevated elsewhere.
- Core operation logic is shared. Mode changes logging/diagnostics only,
  never gameplay outcomes.
- Player commands follow game-state rules; they do not get admin debug
  privileges by default.

---

## 6. Composable testable units

Architecture is three layers. Not every internal function is a Deadworks
`[Command]`.

```text
GameLifecycle  →  composed ops (typed C#, same DLL)  →  service core operations
AdminCommand   →  same core operations (Debug ON)
PlayerCommand  →  same core operations (game-state rules; Clean by default)
```

- Core operations are small, reusable, individually testable units
  (examples: SpawnRift, MoveTeams, CleanupRift, ReturnToDraft).
- Admin and player commands are thin wrappers around core operations.
- Game lifecycle composes operations; individual commands must not own
  the entire match.
- Prefer extracting a stable operation once, then wrapping it — do not
  duplicate working logic into parallel admin/lifecycle paths.

### Plugin classes and services

- One DLL hosts **many small plugin classes** (one per feature, e.g.
  `WorldTextPlugin`, `DraftPlugin`, `RiftPlugin`). The Deadworks loader
  instantiates every `IDeadworksPlugin` type in a DLL.
- Plugin classes are thin hosts: hooks and `[Command]` wrappers only.
  Commands must be declared on a plugin class (the loader only scans
  plugin-class methods).
- Core operations live on **services**: plain C# classes, stateless or held
  in a static inside the assembly, created lazily. Services MUST NOT depend
  on plugin-class load order.
- `Timer` is a per-plugin instance property. Services take an `ITimer`
  parameter from the calling plugin class instead of owning timers.
- Compose with typed C# calls across plugin classes/services inside the
  same DLL. Do **not** compose via `Server.ExecuteCommand("dw_…")` or any
  cross-DLL messaging (separate DLLs are isolated load contexts).
- Each module keeps service files separate from its plugin/commands file,
  so another consumer can include the service alone if command names would
  clash across DLLs.
- Flesh out obvious supporting commands (list, clear, info, where, reset)
  when adding a module or plugin class, and document them in the catalogs.

### Command naming and gating

Names follow SourceMod / CounterStrikeSharp conventions. Target command set:
`RiftRoulette/reference/behavior-inventory.md` §4.

- Every command runs in game as `/name`; Deadworks also registers `!name`
  and `dw_name`.
- Player commands: short verbs, no prefix (`/pick`, `/unpick`, `/picks`,
  `/heroes`, `/status`, `/commands`). `/commands` lists non-hidden names
  without `_`, so player command names must not contain `_`.
- Admin commands: feature word first (`/rift_start`, `/draft_reset`,
  `/player_kick`, `/lobby_setup`, `/session_info`, `/wt_list`, `/mv_tp`,
  `/ent_find`, `/dev_logpath`, `/cleanup_run`).
- Commands that act on another player take a slot number (`<slot>`).
- No aliases: only the names in `behavior-inventory.md` §4 exist.
- Help: the built-in `dw_help` is console-only and lists every visible
  command; players use `/commands` in chat, and `/about` explains the
  mode and betting. Every command sets `Description`. Never name a
  command `help`.
- Every admin command checks `AdminAuth`. A null caller (server console) is
  trusted.
- Command names must not collide across Bublock DLLs (they register
  independently).
- Module commands never take game-specific arguments (teams are team
  numbers, not `sapphire`/`amber`).

---

## 7. Logging and debugging

Debugging is a first-class concern. Follow a recognized structured-logging
shape aligned with Microsoft.Extensions.Logging / OpenTelemetry / Serilog
production practices.

Implementation: `Bublock/Shared/Logging/` (`BublockLog`, `Logger`,
`PlayerRef`; see `Shared/FEATURE.md`). Tested locally by
`Tests/Shared.Tests` (`scripts/test.sh`).

### Where logs live

1. **Server (live write path)** — each DLL writes rolling files to
   `game/bin/win64/bublock/logs/<Dll>/`
   (`Z:\gameserver\server\game\bin\win64\bublock\logs\` on this Windows
   server, `/server/game/bin/win64/bublock/logs/` over SFTP), a sibling of
   `managed/` like the host's `configs/`. The path is resolved from the
   `DeadworksManaged.Api` assembly location (plugin `Assembly.Location` is
   empty). `dw_dev_logpath` (DevTools) prints the resolved path.
2. **Local pull target (Cursor)** — `Bublock/logs/<Dll>/` in this workspace.
   `Bublock/scripts/pull-logs.sh` mirrors newer server files here
   (read-only on the server).

When debugging with Cursor: run `pull-logs.sh` first, then inspect
`Bublock/logs/<Dll>/`. Prefer that over console paste.

### Mandatory

- Do **not** use `Console.WriteLine` for diagnostics. Use
  `BublockLog.Master` / `BublockLog.For("<Feature>")`.
- Console output is allowed only for (a) the logger's single
  `[Bublock.Logging]` line when file writes fail, and (b) command results
  an admin asked for (e.g. DevTools entity listings).
- Never use the old `[Rift Wars]` or `[RiftRumble]` prefixes. The log
  prefix comes from the DLL name (`[RiftRoulette]`).

### Line structure (every entry)

```text
2026-09-26T20:13:00.123Z [Information] [RiftRoulette.Draft] session=a1b2c3d4 round=- player="Theo" steam=76561198192980843 slot=3 | Selected hero Hero=Shiv
```

- ISO-8601 timestamp in **UTC** with milliseconds.
- Severity: `Trace` | `Debug` | `Information` | `Warning` | `Error` | `Critical`
  (align with .NET `LogLevel`).
- Prefix: every feature has its own, `[<Dll>.<Feature>]`
  (e.g. `[RiftRoulette.Draft]`, `[CleanSlate.Cleanup]`,
  `[DevTools.Commands]`). Master lines use `[<Dll>]`.
- Correlation: `session=` always (one per DLL load); `round=` (`-` when no
  round is active).
- Affected player: whenever a line is about a player, pass a `PlayerRef`
  (`controller.ToPlayerRef()`) so the line carries name, Steam ID, and slot.
- Message templates (`{SpawnerIndex}`) rendered as `Name=value` after the
  level check; never string-built before it. `Hero={Hero}` also renders
  `Hero=Shiv` (the formatter does not repeat a name the template wrote).
- Exceptions with stack on `Error` / `Critical`.

### Files (master + one per feature)

```text
# On server under bublock/logs/<Dll>/, and after pull under Bublock/logs/<Dll>/:
  master-YYYYMMDD.log      # game story: startup/shutdown, state transitions,
                           # round start/end, plus every feature Warning+
  <feature>-YYYYMMDD.log   # one per feature: draft, rift, lobby, worldtext,
                           # movement, hud, match, cleanup, commands,
                           # watch, probe, spectate, betting, pause, restart, ...
```

- Every feature gets its own file (`BublockLog.For("Draft")` →
  `draft-YYYYMMDD.log`).
- **Master log** answers: “What happened to the game?” Feature `Warning`+
  lines are copied to master with `(see <feature>.log)`.
- **Feature logs** answer: “What exactly happened inside this subsystem?”
- Master must not dump every low-level diagnostic detail.

### Retention (recent logs prioritized)

- Daily rolling files by UTC date, plus a size roll at 10 MB
  (`<feature>-YYYYMMDD.1.log`, `.2`, ...).
- Retention: files dated more than **7 days** before today are deleted when
  each DLL's logger starts (every load / hot reload).
- Never allow unbounded disk growth.

### Verbosity

- Lifecycle / clean: loggers default to Information+.
- Admin debug invocation: `logger.WithMode(ExecutionMode.Debug)` accepts
  Trace+ for that operation's scope.
- No per-tick spam unless explicitly gated and short-lived.

### Constraints

- Do not invent Deadworks logging APIs; the logger is plain .NET file I/O.
- No extra logging DLLs (e.g. `Microsoft.Extensions.Logging`) in the
  plugins folder; every DLL there is loaded as a plugin.
- Files are closed on `AssemblyLoadContext.Unloading`; writes flush per line.

---

## 8. Deadworks and safety constraints

- Do not invent Deadworks APIs or schema field names. Verify before use
  (schema DB, docs, `lib/` / `ref/`, archive source).
- Prefer isolated changes and one focused test at a time.
- Preserve known-good behavior. Staged refactors; behavior before beauty.
- For small fixes, show exact insertion/replacement sites rather than
  rewriting entire working methods.
- Preserve the known-good KOTH / Rift spawn sequence; do not casually
  alter it.
- Never call `SelectHero` while the player is dead
  (`CanChangeHero`: pawn exists and `IsAlive`).
- Never remove `info_super_trooper_spawn` (crash risk).
- `Entities.All` is an enumerable **property** (not a method).
- `Players.GetAll()` **is** a method.
- Admin authorization: Steam ID from existing DevTools / source only —
  do not invent a Steam ID. Look in `archive/DevTools/` when needed.
  The single shared auth implementation lives in `Bublock/Shared/`.
- EntityIndex is not globally unique across `Entities.All`; do not key
  global dictionaries by EntityIndex alone.

---

## 9. Documentation catalog maintenance

When changing player or admin commands, update:

1. the command’s colocated file doc
2. the owning feature’s `FEATURE.md` (if applicable)
3. `Bublock/RiftRoulette/reference/user-commands.md` or
   `Bublock/RiftRoulette/reference/admin-commands.md`

When changing logging categories, log file names, or resource policy, update
this `.rules` file and the Cursor mirror in the same change.
